max_bits = le16_to_cpu(bg->bg_bits);
if ((offset + ffg->iff_chunksize) > max_bits)
    num_clusters = max_bits - offset;

Reason:
The bug occurs because the non-coherent path (via `ocfs2_read_blocks_sync()`) reads the group descriptor without validating the on-disk `bg_bits` field. A malicious filesystem can set `bg_bits` to a value larger than the actual bitmap capacity (computed from the filesystem format), causing `max_bits` to be used as the scan limit. This leads to out-of-bounds bitmap walking and a use-after-free when `ocfs2_test_bit()` accesses memory beyond the allocated buffer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
max_bitmap_bits = 8 * ocfs2_group_bitmap_size(osb->sb, 0,
                                          osb->s_feature_incompat);
if (max_bits > max_bitmap_bits) {
    mlog(ML_ERROR,
         "Group desc #%llu has %u bits, max bitmap bits %u\n",
         (unsigned long long)blkno, max_bits, max_bitmap_bits);
    max_bits = max_bitmap_bits;
}

Reason:
The fix computes the maximum valid bitmap capacity (`max_bitmap_bits`) based on the filesystem's block size and features. Before using `bg->bg_bits` as the scan limit, it checks if the on-disk value exceeds this capacity. If so, it logs the corruption and clamps `max_bits` to `max_bitmap_bits`, preventing the bitmap walk from accessing memory beyond the buffer. This allows the freefrag scan to continue safely even with corrupt metadata.

